# XSH dlsym()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The probes are the program's own references to the C library: getpid() and getppid() (functions) and
# environ (a data object). RTLD_DEFAULT and RTLD_NEXT are reserved by the page and are not used. A handle that
# is not valid is never passed.

assertion  XSH.dlsym.001
anchor     functions/dlsym.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/dlsym.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       dlsym() on the handle from dlopen(NULL) returns the address of
           getpid() and of getppid(), and calling through it gives the same
           result as calling the function.
note       Which symbols the program image exports is left open by the page, so
           a symbol not found gives UNRESOLVED; a wrong address gives FAIL.
test       t/001-global.c

assertion  XSH.dlsym.002
anchor     functions/dlsym.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       dlsym() of a name that no object defines returns a null pointer, and
           dlerror() then returns a diagnostic.
test       t/002-missing.c

assertion  XSH.dlsym.003
anchor     functions/dlsym.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/dlsym.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       dlsym() on the handle from dlopen() of the pathname of the object
           defining getpid() finds a working getpid() there and finds no symbol
           of an impossible name.
test       t/003-object.c

assertion  XSH.dlsym.004
anchor     functions/dlsym.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/dlsym.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       dlsym() of the data object environ returns the address of a char **
           whose value is environ.
note       The test declares environ itself. Not found gives UNRESOLVED (the set of
           exported symbols is left open).
test       t/004-data.c
